WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:09.273
Maya is a journalist investigating corruption. Sending her evidence now could expose her. Keeping the only usable copy could make it disappear with her.

2
00:00:09.773 --> 00:00:17.987
Canary gives her a third option: keep a file encrypted while she checks in, and hand it only to Noor, the editor she trusts, if she goes silent.

3
00:00:18.487 --> 00:00:30.241
Check-ins travel as shielded Zcash memos, so the public chain shows only encrypted memos. Seven real transactions on Zcash testnet are confirmed, and the page shows them up front.

4
00:00:30.741 --> 00:00:37.717
Here is the browser lab. It sends no transaction, so anyone can safely test every rule. I choose my own file.

5
00:00:38.217 --> 00:00:43.892
I encrypt it locally for one recipient and split the release key into five shares, with three required.

6
00:00:44.392 --> 00:00:46.781
The first signed command registers the vault.

7
00:00:47.281 --> 00:00:50.247
Noor tries to recover the file early. She is blocked.

8
00:00:50.747 --> 00:00:55.184
Maya checks in. Her signed command moves the deadline, and no shares are released.

9
00:00:55.684 --> 00:01:03.343
Every simulated command shows its real counterpart: the matching transaction on Zcash testnet, with a link to the explorer.

10
00:01:03.843 --> 00:01:08.536
The lab's own memo goes through the same verifier that checks the real transactions.

11
00:01:09.036 --> 00:01:18.145
If Maya goes silent, a safety window starts. After it, enough shares are released, and only Noor's matching key recovers the exact original file.

12
00:01:18.645 --> 00:01:27.328
On testnet, shielded transactions carried the real registration, check-in and cancellation commands, and your browser verifies each signature.
